WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.068
-Our first guest tonight
is a Tony, Grammy,

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:05.872
and Emmy Award-winning
performer, singer/songwriter,

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:07.307
and actor you know

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.642
from the critically acclaimed
series "The Politician"

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.545
and Broadway's hit show
"Dear Evan Hansen."

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.482
His sophomore album "Reverie"
is available everywhere now.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:17.583
Please welcome back to the show
Ben Platt.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:19.285
Ben, how are you?

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.619
-Hey, Seth, how you doing?

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:22.188
Nice to see you.
-Nice to see you, too.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.125
It's been five years
since you were last here,

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.861
and it was the beginning
of the Ben Platt era.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.662
It was your first
talk show appearance.

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.165
-It was my first -- Yes, my
first ever late-night moment.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:33.867
I was very, very nervous
and sweaty.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.368
And you looked exactly the same,

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.803
except maybe, like,
a little more sun-kissed.

00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.070
-Thank you very much
for saying that.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.672
And I don't know
if you remember,

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.207
but on your way
out the door that day,

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.176
I said, "By the next time
I see you,

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.812
you better have an Emmy,
a Grammy, and a Tony,"

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:48.614
and you lived up to it.

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:50.582
-And it's only because
you said those things to me

00:00:50.582 --> 00:00:52.384
that any of them happened,
so I really appreciate it.

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:56.255
-I was fishing for that,
and I got you on the hook.

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.857
So, this is really --

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.859
We've talked
when you were here before

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.928
about how you perform a lot
with your family.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.363
That was something
you did growing up.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:08.267
And, you know, like a lot of us,
spent time with family

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.803
over what was a difficult year.

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:13.706
Did you -- In 2020, did you
spend a lot of time with them,

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:17.877
and was that a nice
safety net for you?

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.011
-It was wonderful. Yeah.

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:21.047
I moved home
when the whole thing started

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.682
and moved into
my childhood bedroom

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:25.451
and lived with my parents,

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:28.921
which was a brand new experience
as a pseudo-adult.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.323
And I got to write
my whole album from there,

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:34.093
so I got super inspired by,
like, all of my posters

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:37.664
and decor and yearbooks and all
of the places that I had been.

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:40.933
And I got to, like,
see my parents as people,

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.702
which I feel like is, like,
this weird transition

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:44.270
that happens for all of us
as we get older.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.572
But it was sort of expedited,
I think, for a lot of us

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:48.874
during the pandemic
and certainly for me, too.

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.076
-So, it sounds like

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:53.112
your childhood bedroom
is fairly well preserved?

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:56.916
Would you describe it
as a shrine to Ben's youth?

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.251
-It's pretty untouched.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.085
They didn't necessarily,
like, spruce it in any way,

00:02:00.085 --> 00:02:01.788
but they also didn't,
like, mess with it

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.323
or turn it into a gym,
which I really appreciate.

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:06.892
It's -- We got, like,
all my Broadway posters

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:08.661
like "Pippin"
and "Caroline, or Change,"

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:11.730
and there's a big picture
of me and Beanie Feldstein,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.500
my best friend, from prom.
We went together.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.669
All of my -- You know,
all of my two soccer trophies

00:02:17.669 --> 00:02:20.206
from when I was
like 7 years old,

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.907
couple of my bar mitzvah
centerpieces,

00:02:21.907 --> 00:02:23.409
which were also
giant "Playbills."

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.411
So it's a very
theater-friendly room.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:26.879
The one thing
that I never identified with

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.816
is that my wallpaper's, like, a
bunch of old, like, cartography.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.350
Like, it's a bunch of maps.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.419
And it was 'cause I went away
to camp one summer,

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:34.888
and my mom was like,
"As your birthday gift,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.221
when you get home from camp,

00:02:36.221 --> 00:02:38.791
I'm going to make your room,
like, mature and older,

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.059
'cause you're going
to middle school,

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.995
and I want you to feel like
you're growing up."

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.462
And I was like,
"Oh, cool, awesome."

00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:44.898
And then
her interpretation of that

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:46.365
was to give me, like,
a black comforter

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:47.667
and, like, maps
all over the room.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:51.103
So I was like,
"Do you know me at all, Mom?"

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:52.939
But it's very untouched.

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.274
-She's like,
"Based on everything

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:55.374
you've shared with me, Ben,

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:57.644
I believe your future
is sea travel."

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.411
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:01.580
Exactly. And how right she was.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.750
-I think my family, my parents,

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.086
half-preserved
my childhood bedroom

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:09.489
and then added a work desk

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:14.126
that has the oldest printer
you've ever seen.

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:18.797
Like, they made this decision
in the mid-2010s,

00:03:18.797 --> 00:03:22.101
and yet they somehow found
a printer from like the '80s.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:24.137
-Well, I bet aesthetically, it's
pretty nice looking, at least.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:26.539
-It's beautiful, and loud,
which is really nice.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.274
-That's what you want
from your electronics --

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:30.209
as disruptive as possible.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:33.112
-You mentioned Beanie,
and, you know, you mentioned

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.481
having this really strong
friend support system

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.749
the last time we talked.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.450
When you work on
something like an album,

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.719
does that come into play?

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:42.421
Are you lucky to have people
to bounce stuff off?

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:45.458
-Absolutely, especially
Noah Galvin, who is my boyfriend

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.427
and who I spent most
of the pandemic with, as well.

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:50.296
You know, he -- Getting to
play the music for him

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:53.132
and let it just be kind of
something just for us,

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.701
for my friends and for my family

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.736
and, you know,
see how much they love it

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.404
and listen to it
in their own lives,

00:03:58.404 --> 00:03:59.705
like, leading up to the release

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:02.541
definitely relieves
a lot of the anxiety and worry

00:04:02.541 --> 00:04:04.277
that comes with
putting something out

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:07.146
that has so many little personal
nuggets and experiences in it,

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.415
that you, you know, worry
if people will understand

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.051
or connect to or feel like
they see themselves in.

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:13.452
So for the people
that matter the most

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:15.955
to already be kind of listening
to it and jamming to it

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:19.525
and appreciating it, it makes
that feel smaller in a good way.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.694
-I can imagine, too,
you can constantly tinker

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.462
with something like an album
before you put it out.

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:25.864
Do you have now a sense
of relief that that part is over

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:28.133
and that it's out
for people to enjoy?

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:30.336
-So much so. Yeah.
I think, like I always --

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.471
Or I guess I've only had this
experience really one other time

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:33.839
when I put out my first album.

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:36.208
But, of course, in the days
leading up to the release,

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:38.678
I was listening
pretty obsessively

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:40.846
and just trying to really --

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.180
You know,
before you put anything out,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.983
I feel like you want to
really feel like you know it

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:46.285
and you know exactly what it is
that's about to be in the world

00:04:46.285 --> 00:04:47.719
before it belongs
to everybody else.

00:04:47.719 --> 00:04:50.089
'Cause I find
that once it's everyone else's,

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.057
I'm not as inclined
to listen on my own.

00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:54.426
So I was kind of, like, soaking
up the end of that experience.

00:04:54.426 --> 00:04:57.229
And of course,
I find little, nitpicky things.

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:58.965
And, you know, as you grow

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.100
even since, you know,
things have been finalized

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.269
and mastered,
things still change in life,

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.304
and you have new perspectives
and new ideas.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:08.241
But in terms of as a snapshot
of when it was made

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.809
and what part of my life
I was in,

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:11.977
I do feel it's
a good example of that.

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:13.680
And I feel very
authentically me.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.982
So, I'm happy with
where it ended up.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.718
-I imagine you have a whole new
relationship with an album

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:21.020
when you go out
and start touring with it,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.655
which you've just announced
you're going to do.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.424
Is that -- I mean, just
in general for someone like you

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:27.760
to get in front of an audience
again must be exciting,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.096
but also to do it
with this really personal work

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:32.464
must be cool.

00:05:32.464 --> 00:05:35.734
-Absolutely. I feel like
much like any other performer

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.904
during this, you know, horror,
two years, terrible time,

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:41.740
I've been, like, really just
craving that live experience

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.843
and having that connection
with people in the room.

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:45.445
And it's what I grew up doing.

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:48.113
It's how I started
working and how --

00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:50.249
sort of my comfort zone,
my favorite place to be.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:52.151
And so, yeah, the idea of --

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.253
I think always
when I'm recording music

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.055
or putting together an album,
in the back of my head,

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:59.525
the end goal is always the live
experience and the singing live.

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.327
And so at the end of the day,

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:02.861
these are all meant
to really be performed.

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:04.897
And so I think
they only really feel like

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:05.965
they've reached the finish line

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.266
and they're, like,
fully realized

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:10.536
when I get to actually sing them
for live human beings.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.638
-Not -- You know,
touring is one thing.

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:15.340
You know, another that you have
an intense relationship with

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.409
is obviously Broadway.

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:21.114
Do you look more forward
to being onstage again one day

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:22.815
or just the simple pleasure

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:26.885
of being back in a Broadway
audience as a, you know, viewer?

00:06:26.885 --> 00:06:28.387
-Honestly, equally.

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:31.123
I mean, I think first, I'd love
to, like, rip the Band-Aid off

00:06:31.123 --> 00:06:33.225
by being a patron
and an audience member,

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:35.794
'cause that's, like, my favorite
activity in the world.

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:39.332
And I think I -- That's like
my favorite thing to do

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:41.000
to just, you know, process.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.469
Or any time I need
any kind of relief

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:44.737
when I was living in New York

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:46.005
for the last, you know,
eight or nine years,

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.007
it was always to try
and go see a Broadway show.

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:51.010
And I think,
much like going to restaurants

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.911
and just so many things
that felt so small

00:06:52.911 --> 00:06:55.648
or so ordinary to us
now feel like little adventures

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:57.282
and, like, roller coasters
every time we do them.

00:06:57.282 --> 00:07:00.052
And I think to the nth degree,
seeing a Broadway show

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.855
is going to feel like
this entirely new experience.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:06.292
So I'm very hungry to do that
and then, of course, after that,

00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:07.960
get back up there myself
for sure.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.596
-We're also very excited
that you're sticking around

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.265
to do a song from the new album.

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:13.832
Thank you so much for that.

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:16.435
And congrats on the album,

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.004
and thanks for being here.

00:07:18.004 --> 00:07:19.405
-Thank you so much.
Thanks for having me,

00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:20.706
and thanks for
letting me sing for you.

